# $HOME is this repo's work tree, so ignore everything by default. Otherwise an
# `add -A` sweeps the whole home directory into this public repo — caches, shell
# history, and real secrets like .cargo/credentials and .claude/.credentials.json.
#
# Git ignores nothing that's already tracked, so the tracked files need no entry
# here; this list is only about what happens to *new* files. It opts in to the
# directories where anything new should be picked up automatically. Everywhere
# else, start tracking a new file explicitly:
#
#     dotfiles add -f ~/.newrc
#
# Deliberately NOT opted in: ~/.ssh (keys), ~/.local/bin (mixed with pip and npm
# console shims), and new top-level dotfiles — those should be a conscious choice.
#
# Git never looks inside an ignored directory, so re-including a nested one takes
# three steps: un-ignore the directory, re-ignore its contents with `/dir/*`,
# then un-ignore the specific children.

/*

# Wholly ours.
!/.github/

# Everything else in ~/.claude is local state: credentials, chat history, caches.
!/.claude/
/.claude/*
!/.claude/commands/
!/.claude/scripts/
!/.claude/skills/

# Most of ~/.config belongs to other programs. Denying by default also keeps the
# untracked local.* overrides out, which is their whole point.
!/.config/
/.config/*
!/.config/bacon/
!/.config/git/

# ~/.vim is nearly all ours, but vim-plug installs into plugged/ and netrw
# writes .netrwhist.
!/.vim/
/.vim/*
!/.vim/autoload/
!/.vim/colors/
!/.vim/doc/
!/.vim/ftdetect/
!/.vim/ftplugin/
!/.vim/plugin/
!/.vim/syntax/
